\section{Motivation and significance}
Repository-level AI coding tools use test results when deciding whether an edit is satisfactory. SWE-bench and SWE-agent make this interaction a concrete research setting \cite{jimenez2024swebench,yang2024sweagent}. A producer may validate a patch before handing the workspace to another tool or reviewer. The consumer then needs to distinguish an identified earlier success from newly executed evidence: the same shell command can observe different source, dependencies, or external state.

ZeroRun exposes the previous successful exit status of a deterministic test command under an unchanged declared execution identity. Its users are testing-tool integrators and researchers evaluating validation handoffs. A consumer that only needs the earlier status can request reuse; one needing a new transcript executes fresh. This makes the benefit and cost of each choice measurable. ZeroRun neither learns a caching policy nor predicts patch correctness.

Regression-test selection avoids tests considered unaffected by a change \cite{rothermel1997safe,yoo2012regression}. Existing build and LLM-tool caches already address result reuse (Table~\ref{tab:contracts}); eidos provides adjacent LLM function-integration software \cite{aldanamartin2025eidos}. LabChain combines hash-based intermediate-result reuse with modular scientific pipelines and JSON configuration \cite{couto2026labchain}. ZeroRun instead exposes an earlier test-status decision under an operator-qualified execution identity. Neither content hashing nor attaching a cache to an AI interface is a new algorithm here.

The contribution is an inspectable validation handoff: a repository-bound status interface, a hit path that avoids source staging, and an executable evaluation kit linking declared input boundaries, independent fresh outcomes, and full request cost. Its unit is an operator-qualified, result-only test task. Integrators can separately inspect agreement, eligibility, and practical cost without treating a cache hit as a fresh transcript.

